<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Wittelsbach</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wittelsbach dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Wittelsbach is a German dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, as duchy, electorate and kingdom, from 1180 to 1918 and the Electoral Palatinate from 1214. Its members included Emperors Louis IV and Charles VII and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and for a time it held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, Holland, Hainaut and Zeeland. The last king of Bavaria, Ludwig III, was deposed in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach.jpg" alt="House of Wittelsbach" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armoiries_Bavi%C3%A8re.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origins of the family lie with the Counts of Scheyern, themselves of uncertain origins. When Otto I, Count of Scheyern, died in 1072, his third son Otto II acquired Wittelsbach Castle, near Aichach. In 1119 the counts left Scheyern Castle, built around 940, for Wittelsbach, and gave the former to monks to found Scheyern Abbey. Some have speculated that the family descends from Margrave Henry of Schweinfurt and his father Berthold, and perhaps from the Luitpolding dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in the 10th century, but their background is itself disputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Wittelsbachs ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1180 to 1918 and the Palatinate from 1214 to 1805, succeeding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a> in both. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> gave the counts palatine the electoral dignity, and the Duchy of Bavaria became an electorate in 1623. The Bavarian branch kept the duchy until it died out in 1777. The Palatinate branch then took Bavaria as well. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> later raised Bavaria to a kingdom, which lasted until 1918. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> of 9 February 1801 Bavaria lost the Palatinate and the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a> and Jülich, and it allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>Members of the family also ruled beyond the Empire. Duke Otto III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>, a maternal grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a>, was elected anti-king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> as Béla V from 1305 and gave up the throne by 1308. Christopher reigned over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1440 to 1448, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xi-of-sweden">Charles XI</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> and Ulrika Eleonora ruled Sweden from 1654 to 1720. Frederick V, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Winter King</a>. In 1699 the death of Joseph Ferdinand, a son of Maximilian II Emanuel, ended the chance that the family would inherit the Spanish throne, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> had chosen him as heir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-of-hanover">Sophia of Hanover</a>, born a Wittelsbach princess of the Palatinate, inherited the Stuart succession rights under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> of 1701. She died two months before Queen Anne, and her eldest son became George I in 1714, so the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a> inherited the British crown. Wittelsbach archbishops, most of them of the Bavarian branch, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> between 1597 and 1794. Other members included Elisabeth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, known as Sisi, Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a>.</p><p>In the Nazi period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupprecht-crown-prince-of-bavaria">Crown Prince Rupprecht</a> earned Hitler's enmity by opposing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> of 1923. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1938 and went into hiding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> after German forces occupied the country in September 1943.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Wittelsbach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hainaut passed through several dynasties: the house of Flanders, then the Avesnes, who also held Holland and Zeeland from 1299, then the <b>Wittelsbachs</b> from 1345 and the Valois dukes of Burgundy from 1433, before the Habsburgs inherited it in 1482.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Wittelsbachs</b> the duchy's wars were often internal: partitions led to fraternal conflicts, and the Landshut War of Succession of 1503–1505 ended only through imperial arbitration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Wittelsbachs</b> held it until 1803.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Bavaria was proclaimed on 1 January 1806, when Elector Maximilian IV Joseph, of the <b>Wittelsbach dynasty</b> that had ruled Bavaria since 1180, took the title King Maximilian I Joseph.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ascanian line died out in 1320 the Mark passed to the <b>Wittelsbachs</b> in 1323 and to the Luxembourgs in 1373.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Holland passed to the Avesnes counts of Hainaut in 1299, the <b>Wittelsbachs</b> in 1345 and the Burgundian State in 1433, amid the Hook and Cod wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
